            <div xml:id="d213">

                <pb facs="0182" n="176" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg176"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Tientsin/245: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Dooman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-06-10T15:00:00+09:00">June 10, 1939—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received June 11—8:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>267. Department’s 156, June 8, 3 p.m. I spoke today to the Vice Minister for
                    Foreign Affairs<anchor n="63" xml:id="d213-f1"/> along the lines desired by the
                    Department. Sawada expressed regret that the attitude reflected by the British
                    officials in Tientsin “left no room” for conciliatory discussions with them by
                    the Japanese. He added however that Caldwell enjoyed the confidence of both
                    sides and that “it would be helpful if he could use his good offices.”</p>
                <p>Repeated to Chungking, Tientsin, Shanghai.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dooman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d213-f1" n="63" xml:id="d213-f1-content">
                <p>Renzo Sawada.</p>
